{
  "id": "ep092",
  "duration": 53.936,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.398,
      "text": "A distant star seems to shine with short-lived radioactive elements.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Johannes Hevelius - Centaurus.jpg",
        "credit": "Johannes Hevelius",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Hevelius_-_Centaurus.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 2.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 2.0,
          "end": 4.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.398,
      "end": 10.543,
      "text": "We expect stars to be made of hydrogen, with atmospheres containing common metals like iron.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Arrhenius - L\u2019\u00e9volution des mondes, 1910 - Fig-59.jpg",
        "credit": "Svante August Arrhenius / Th\u00e9ophile Seyrig",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrhenius_-_L%E2%80%99%C3%A9volution_des_mondes,_1910_-_Fig-59.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.4,
          "end": 10.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.543,
      "end": 18.563,
      "text": "In 1961, astronomer Antoni Przybylski analyzed a star 356 light-years away.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Antoni Przybylski SLNSW FL16054294.jpg",
        "credit": "unknown",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoni_Przybylski_SLNSW_FL16054294.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.533,
          "end": 13.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 13.667,
          "end": 18.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.563,
      "end": 26.458,
      "text": "Its spectrum showed almost no iron, but was swamped by rare-earth elements thousands of times more abundant than in our Sun.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lantanoidat karjalakse.png",
        "credit": "Fembriha",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lantanoidat_karjalakse.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.567,
          "end": 26.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.458,
      "end": 35.078,
      "text": "Weirder still, early reports identified promethium, a radioactive element with a half-life of only 17.7 years.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Promethium (Element - 61) 1.jpg",
        "credit": "James St. John",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Promethium_(Element_-_61)_1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.467,
          "end": 30.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 30.767,
          "end": 35.067,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 35.078,
      "end": 39.823,
      "text": "In a billion-year-old star, these elements should have decayed away eons ago.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Art Institute of Chicago (CC0)",
        "title": "Eight Point Star Tile with a Phoenix",
        "credit": "Iran\nProbably Kashan",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/92287"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 35.067,
          "end": 37.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 37.933,
          "end": 39.833,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.823,
      "end": 47.923,
      "text": "While some blame alien nuclear waste, others believe crowded stellar light lines simply mimic this impossible signal.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Cloaked in stars (potw1422a).tiff",
        "credit": "J.Colosimo (www.colosimophotography.com)/ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloaked_in_stars_(potw1422a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.833,
          "end": 45.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 45.433,
          "end": 47.933,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 47.923,
      "end": 53.586,
      "text": "But if this radioactive waste is real, it must be constantly replenished on this bizarre star.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Johannes Hevelius - Centaurus.jpg",
        "credit": "Johannes Hevelius",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Hevelius_-_Centaurus.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 47.933,
          "end": 53.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
